\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Dataset.}
We conduct experiments on DocRED~\citep{yao2019docred}, a large-scale document-level relation extraction benchmark containing 3,053 training and 998 development documents spanning 96 relation types. Each document requires identifying relational triples across multiple sentences and grounding each triple with supporting evidence sentences. We use Re-DocRED~\citep{tan2022redocred} for cross-dataset generalization evaluation.

\paragraph{Models.}
Our primary model is Qwen3-4B. To study scale effects, we additionally train Qwen3-1.7B and Qwen3-8B under identical configurations. We include LLaMA-3.1-8B as a cross-family validation point.

\paragraph{Training.}
All models use LoRA~\citep{hu2022lora} with rank $r{=}64$ and $\alpha{=}128$ over a structured output format that jointly produces relation triples and evidence sentences. For RSFT, we sample $N{=}8$ candidate outputs per document and select the top-$k{=}1$ response ranked by CED scoring (\S\ref{sec:ced}), which combines NLI-based evidence faithfulness with extraction accuracy. GRPO experiments use group sizes $G{=}4$ (QLoRA, 4-bit) and $G{=}8$ (bf16 full-precision LoRA). DPO constructs preference pairs from CED-ranked outputs.

\paragraph{Metrics.}
We report five metrics. \textbf{Ign-F1} is the primary DocRED metric that ignores triples appearing in the training set, matching the official leaderboard protocol. \textbf{Rel-F1} measures relation extraction F1 on all triples. \textbf{Evi-F1 (TP-only)} measures evidence sentence F1 conditioned on correctly predicted triples, reflecting evidence quality independent of extraction errors. \textbf{Evi-F1 (joint)} includes false-positive and false-negative triples in the denominator following the DREEAM protocol~\citep{ma2023dreeam}, enabling cross-method evidence comparison. \textbf{EDCR} (Evidence-Decoupling Ratio) is our proposed diagnostic: the ratio of evidence recall on correctly-predicted triples to overall extraction recall, where values substantially exceeding the SFT baseline indicate RL-induced decoupling. All primary results use the development set; test set submission is pending.


\subsection{Main Results}
\label{sec:main_results}

Table~\ref{tab:main} presents the primary comparison between SFT, no-evidence SFT, and RSFT CED on Qwen3-4B. The SFT baseline achieves 39.74 Ign-F1 with joint evidence generation. Removing evidence from the output format entirely (no-evidence SFT) improves extraction to 47.21 Ign-F1, a 7.47-point gap that quantifies the \emph{evidence tax} at the 4B scale. RSFT CED achieves a four-seed mean of 44.66 Ign-F1 (+4.92 over SFT, standard deviation 0.49) while maintaining evidence quality (Evi-F1 81.15 vs.\ 79.86 for SFT). The EDCR decreases from 0.6982 to 0.6503, confirming tighter coupling between extraction and evidence capabilities. Individual seeds (42, 43, 44, 45) yield Ign-F1 of 44.23, 45.12, 45.11, and 44.16 respectively, confirming low variance across runs.

The remaining gap to the no-evidence upper bound (2.09 points) represents the residual cost of producing structured evidence, a cost that shrinks at larger scales as we show in Section~\ref{sec:scaling}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Main results on DocRED dev set (Qwen3-4B). Four-seed mean is the primary result (std 0.49). Individual seeds shown for transparency. Best Ign-F1 in \textbf{bold}; best among evidence-generating methods \underline{underlined}.}
\label{tab:main}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Ign-F1} & \textbf{Rel-F1} & \textbf{Evi-F1} & \textbf{EDCR} \\
\midrule
SFT baseline & 39.74 & 0.4049 & 79.86 & 0.6982 \\
SFT no-evidence & \textbf{47.21} & \textbf{0.4876} & -- & -- \\
\midrule
RSFT CED (s42) & 44.23 & 0.4517 & 81.74 & 0.6569 \\
RSFT CED (s43) & \underline{45.12} & \underline{0.4601} & 80.63 & 0.6542 \\
RSFT CED (s44) & 45.11 & 0.4621 & 80.50 & 0.6350 \\
RSFT CED (s45) & 44.16 & 0.4514 & 81.74 & 0.6550 \\
\midrule
RSFT CED (mean) & 44.66 & 0.4563 & 81.15 & 0.6503 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


\subsection{Evidence-Extraction Decoupling Spectrum}
\label{sec:spectrum}

Different RL algorithms produce qualitatively different failure modes when applied to joint evidence-and-extraction generation. We organize these outcomes along a \emph{decoupling spectrum} ranging from catastrophic collapse to mild perturbation, shown in Table~\ref{tab:spectrum} (visualized in Figure~\ref{fig:spectrum}, Appendix).

\paragraph{GRPO QLoRA: catastrophic collapse.}
Under QLoRA quantization ($G{=}4$), GRPO drives Rel-F1 to 0.0138 (near zero extraction) while Evi-F1 rises to 85.04 and EDCR reaches 0.9876. The model learns to produce well-formed evidence sentences but loses the ability to predict correct relation triples. This extreme decoupling occurs because 4-bit quantization constrains the effective parameter space, forcing the reward signal to optimize whichever sub-task yields easier gradient progress. Evidence sentence generation, being closer to extractive summarization, wins this competition.

\paragraph{GRPO bf16: mild, non-monotonic.}
Full-precision GRPO ($G{=}8$) avoids catastrophe. Its training trajectory shows non-monotonic Rel-F1 fluctuation (0.434 at step 30, 0.428 at step 50, 0.434 at step 70, 0.455 at step 100), stabilizing near the SFT baseline. The best checkpoint achieves Rel-F1 0.4549 with Evi-F1 80.86 and EDCR 0.6318. The decoupling is mild: bf16 precision preserves enough capacity for both sub-tasks to improve jointly, though the non-monotonic trajectory suggests fragile optimization dynamics that require careful checkpoint selection.

\paragraph{DPO: gradual decay.}
DPO preference learning shows a progressive pattern. At 1 epoch, Rel-F1 remains at 0.4034 with Evi-F1 80.88 and EDCR 0.7139. Extended training (3 epochs) degrades extraction to 0.3429 while evidence improves to 84.03\footnote{DPO 3-epoch results use the pre-D076 evaluation protocol; included for trend analysis.}, with EDCR rising to 0.7294. Unlike GRPO's sudden collapse, DPO gradually trades extraction quality for evidence quality as training progresses.

\paragraph{RSFT: no decoupling.}
RSFT avoids the decoupling problem entirely. By selecting high-quality complete outputs (jointly good extraction and evidence) rather than applying gradient-based reward maximization, RSFT maintains both capabilities simultaneously (mean Rel-F1 0.4563, Evi-F1 81.15, EDCR 0.6503). The key insight is that rejection sampling operates on discrete outputs rather than differentiable reward signals, so it cannot exploit the extraction-evidence trade-off that gradient methods find.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Evidence-extraction decoupling across RL paradigms (Qwen3-4B). Severity indicates the degree of extraction collapse relative to evidence improvement.}
\label{tab:spectrum}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Paradigm} & \textbf{Config} & \textbf{Rel-F1} & \textbf{Evi-F1} & \textbf{EDCR} & \textbf{Severity} \\
\midrule
GRPO & QLoRA $G{=}4$ & 0.0138 & 85.04 & 0.9876 & Catastrophic \\
GRPO & bf16 $G{=}8$ & 0.4549 & 80.86 & 0.6318 & Mild \\
DPO & bf16 1ep & 0.4034 & 80.88 & 0.7139 & Gradual \\
DPO & bf16 3ep$^\dagger$ & 0.3429 & 84.03 & 0.7294 & Gradual \\
\midrule
SFT & baseline & 0.4049 & 79.86 & 0.6982 & None \\
RSFT & CED (mean) & 0.4563 & 81.15 & 0.6503 & None \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{-2mm}
{\raggedright\footnotesize $^\dagger$Pre-D076 evaluation protocol.\par}
\end{table}


\subsection{Scale-Dependent Evidence Tax}
\label{sec:scaling}

The evidence tax, defined as the Ign-F1 difference between no-evidence and joint-evidence SFT, varies non-monotonically across model scales. Table~\ref{tab:scaling} presents this analysis across four models (Figure~\ref{fig:scaling} in Appendix).

At the 4B scale, the evidence tax is severe: $-7.47$ points. Generating evidence sentences alongside relation triples consumes capacity that would otherwise improve extraction. At 8B, the tax nearly vanishes ($-0.28$ points for Qwen3-8B, $-0.68$ for LLaMA-3.1-8B), indicating that 8B-class models have sufficient capacity to handle both tasks simultaneously. The most surprising result appears at 1.7B: instead of paying a larger tax, the smallest model \emph{benefits} from evidence generation (+8.04 points). On a fair evaluation subset that controls for output format effects (see Appendix), this benefit is +6.2 points. We hypothesize that for small models, the evidence generation task provides implicit regularization, forcing the model to attend to supporting context rather than memorizing surface-level patterns.

RSFT improves all scales. The gain is +2.64 for 1.7B, +4.92 for 4B, and +3.82 for 8B, confirming that CED-based selection generalizes across model capacities. The 4B model benefits most because it has the largest evidence tax to recover from. A striking consequence of this pattern is that 1.7B RSFT achieves 50.50 Ign-F1, nearly matching 8B SFT at 50.51. CED-based rejection sampling enables a 1.7B model to rival a 4.7$\times$ larger model on evidence-grounded extraction, with direct implications for deployment cost: practitioners can substitute a small model with RSFT for a large model with vanilla SFT at comparable accuracy.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Scale-dependent evidence tax and RSFT gain. Evidence tax = SFT with evidence minus SFT no-evidence (positive means evidence helps). All results on DocRED dev set Ign-F1.}
\label{tab:scaling}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{SFT w/ evi} & \textbf{No-evi} & \textbf{RSFT} & \textbf{Evi Tax} & \textbf{RSFT Gain} \\
\midrule
Qwen3-1.7B & 47.86 & 39.82 & 50.50 & +8.04 & +2.64 \\
Qwen3-4B & 39.74 & 47.21 & 44.66 & $-$7.47 & +4.92 \\
Qwen3-8B & 50.51 & 50.79 & \textbf{54.33} & $-$0.28 & +3.82 \\
LLaMA-3.1-8B & 53.84 & 54.52 & 55.58 & $-$0.68 & +1.74 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


\subsection{Evidence Strategy Comparison}
\label{sec:evidence_strategy}

We compare joint generation against post-hoc retrieval, using Evi-F1 (joint) with the DREEAM-protocol denominator~\citep{ma2023dreeam} for fair cross-strategy comparison. Post-hoc BM25 retrieval (entity-constrained, $k{=}2$ sentences per triple) achieves higher Rel-F1 (0.4876) by avoiding the evidence tax, but produces lower Evi-F1 (joint) of 0.347 with EDCR 0.766. RSFT CED (s43) reaches Evi-F1 (joint) 0.361 with EDCR 0.654, a 1.4-point evidence advantage and 0.112 EDCR reduction. CED reranking at inference time (selecting the best of $N{=}8$ outputs by CED score) achieves Rel-F1 0.4864 and Evi-F1 81.88 (TP-only), approaching no-evidence extraction quality while preserving evidence. Joint training produces more faithful evidence grounded in the extraction reasoning rather than retrieved post-hoc; inference-time reranking complements this by recovering extraction accuracy.


\subsection{Generalization}
\label{sec:generalization}

Cross-architecture validation on LLaMA-3.1-8B confirms the scaling patterns observed with Qwen3: the evidence tax at 8B is near zero ($-0.68$ points), and RSFT CED yields +1.74 Ign-F1 gain. Cross-dataset evaluation on Re-DocRED~\citep{tan2022redocred} shows robust evidence transfer (Evi-F1 81.7, within 1 point of DocRED) despite a 3.15-point Ign-F1 drop from domain shift. Our best joint Evi-F1 (0.36, DREEAM-protocol denominator) falls below supervised DREEAM (54.01\%~\citep{ma2023dreeam}), a gap driven by extraction performance rather than evidence quality (TP-only Evi-F1 exceeds 80\%). Full generalization results appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:generalization_full}.

\section{Limitations}

All results use the DocRED development set; test set submission is pending. Exact-match F1 penalizes surface-form-different entity mentions~\citep{wan2024genres}, likely underestimating absolute extraction capability, though relative comparisons remain valid.

We evaluate one algorithm per RL family (GRPO, DPO); other algorithms may yield different results. CED scoring depends on an external NLI model, and the RSFT ablation uses limited seeds (four for CED, two for flat-NLI). The evidence tax pattern observed up to 8B may not extrapolate to larger scales.

