\section{Limitations}

All results use the DocRED development set; test set submission is pending. Exact-match F1 penalizes surface-form-different entity mentions~\citep{wan2024genres}, likely underestimating absolute extraction capability, though relative comparisons remain valid.

We evaluate one algorithm per RL family (GRPO, DPO); other algorithms may yield different results. CED scoring depends on an external NLI model, and the RSFT ablation uses limited seeds (four for CED, two for flat-NLI). The evidence tax pattern observed up to 8B may not extrapolate to larger scales.

We compare joint generation against post-hoc retrieval, using Evi-F1 (joint) with the DREEAM-protocol denominator~\citep{ma2023dreeam} for fair cross-strategy comparison. Post-hoc BM25 retrieval (entity-constrained, $k{=}2$ sentences per triple) achieves higher Rel-F1 (0.4876) by avoiding the evidence tax, but produces lower Evi-F1 (joint) of 0.347 with EDCR 0.766. RSFT CED (s43) reaches Evi-F1 (joint) 0.361 with EDCR 0.654, a 1.4-point evidence advantage and 0.112 EDCR reduction. CED reranking at inference time (selecting the best of $N{=}8$ outputs by CED score) achieves Rel-F1 0.4864 and Evi-F1 81.88 (TP-only), approaching no-evidence extraction quality while preserving evidence. Joint training produces more faithful evidence grounded in the extraction reasoning rather than retrieved post-hoc; inference-time reranking complements this by recovering extraction accuracy.


\subsection{Generalization}
\label{sec:generalization}

Cross-architecture validation on LLaMA-3.1-8B confirms the scaling patterns observed with Qwen3: the evidence tax at 8B is near zero ($-0.68$ points), and RSFT CED yields +1.74 Ign-F1 gain. Cross-dataset evaluation on Re-DocRED~\citep{tan2022redocred} shows robust evidence transfer (Evi-F1 81.7, within 1 point of DocRED) despite a 3.15-point Ign-F1 drop from domain shift. Our best joint Evi-F1 (0.36, DREEAM-protocol denominator) falls below supervised DREEAM (54.01\%~\citep{ma2023dreeam}), a gap driven by extraction performance rather than evidence quality (TP-only Evi-F1 exceeds 80\%). Full generalization results appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:generalization_full}.

